</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">December 10, 2008</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible complex analytic space with <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> an immersion of a smooth Zariski open subset, and let <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact Kähler. Then provided the local monodromy operators at infinity are quasi-unipotent, <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> is known to carry a pure Hodge structure of weight <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+n</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> carries a mixed Hodge structure of weight <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq k+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq k+n</annotation></semantics></math>. In this note it is shown that the image of the natural map <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the lowest weight part of this mixed Hodge structure. In the algebraic case this easily follows from the formalism of mixed sheaves, but the analytic case is rather complicated, in particular when the complement <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-U</annotation></semantics></math> is not a hypersurface.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a compact Kähler manifold <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> the decomposition of complex valued <math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> differential <math id="Sx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-forms into types induces the Hodge decomposition for the de Rham group <math id="Sx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> equipping this group with a pure weight <math id="Sx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge structure. For singular or non-compact complex analytic spaces this is no longer true in general. For instance <math id="Sx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> has rank <math id="Sx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> while it should have even rank if it would carry a weight <math id="Sx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Cohomology groups of <em id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">algebraic</em> varieties instead carry a canonical <em id="Sx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed Hodge structure</em>, i.e. there is a rationally defined increasing weight filtration so that the <math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th graded pieces carry a weight <math id="Sx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge structure. In the above example there is only one weight, namely <math id="Sx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\mathbb{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight <math id="Sx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, Deligne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del71</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del74</a>]</cite> constructed a good functorial theory for the cohomology of algebraic varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a smooth variety <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> the weight filtration can be seen on the level of forms as follows. First choose a so-called <em id="Sx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">good</em> compactification, i.e. a smooth (projective) compactification <math id="Sx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Sx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=X-U</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor with normal crossings. De Rham cohomology of <math id="Sx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the cohomology of the full complex of smooth forms on <math id="Sx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> but it can also be calculated using the subcomplex of rational forms having at most logarithmic poles along <math id="Sx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and the weight filtration is given by the number of logarithmic poles. Indeed, <math id="Sx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> carries a mixed Hodge structure with <math id="Sx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k-1}H^{k}(U)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k-1}H^{k}(U)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and where <math id="Sx1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of the restriction <math id="Sx1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X)\to H^{k}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X)\to H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the analytic case, a similar assertion holds provided a Kähler compactification <math id="Sx1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> exists. All of these assertions are well known consequences of Deligne’s theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the analytic category we work with manifolds <math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> Zariski-open in some compact Kähler space <math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For these, <em id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">good compactifications</em> exist as in the algebraic case<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          More details can be found in § <a href="#S3" title="3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></span></span>. The weight filtration of the mixed Hodge structure on <math id="Sx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> may (and indeed does) depend on the compactification as shown by the following example.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexmplex1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmple">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexmplex1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example</span></span><span id="Thmexmplex1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexmplex1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexmplex1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U={\mathbb{C}}^{*}\times{\mathbb{C}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U={\mathbb{C}}^{*}\times{\mathbb{C}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> can be <em id="Thmexmplex1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">analytically</em> compactified in two ways: one is <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, a second one is the compactification <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> which is the total space of the <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on an elliptic curve <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the non-trivial extension of a trivial line bundle by a trivial line bundle. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">P-S</a>, Example 4.19]</cite>. The first has <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}H^{1}(U)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}H^{1}(U)=0</annotation></semantics></math> while the second has <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}H^{1}(U)\simeq H^{1}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}H^{1}(U)\simeq H^{1}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. Deligne’s results imply that this would not happen if <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexmplex1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> can be dominated by a third smooth projective compactification: indeed, two <em id="Thmexmplex1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">birationally</em> equivalent compactifications would give the same mixed Hodge structures.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the analytic category we are thus led to introduce the notion of bimeromorphic equivalence: two smooth Kähler compactifications <math id="Sx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> are said to be <em id="Sx1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bimeromorphically equivalent</em> if they are dominated by a third smooth Kähler compactification <math id="Sx1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. If, moreover, the dominating bimeromorphic morphisms <math id="Sx1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> are projective, we say that <math id="Sx1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="Sx1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">projective-bimeromorphically equivalent</em>. So <math id="Sx1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> always has a good compactification projectively bimeromorphically equivalent to <math id="Sx1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, but there may be other good compactifications which are not even bimeromorphically equivalent to <math id="Sx1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as our example shows. However, Deligne’s results imply that for any good Kähler compactification <math id="Sx1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> we have that <math id="Sx1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}H^{k}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}H^{k}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="Sx1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, in our example, one still has that <math id="Sx1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}H^{1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}H^{1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="Sx1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the compactification in both cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One can generalize the discussion to cohomology with values in locally constant coefficients where Deligne’s theory does not apply. To motivate why one should consider these,
look at the Leray spectral sequence for a morphism <math id="Sx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> between compact Kähler spaces; these involve the terms <math id="Sx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q}(X,R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}(X,R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>. Assuming that there is a non-empty Zariski-open subset <math id="Sx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> over which <math id="Sx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth, the sheaf <math id="Sx1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed locally constant and its fibers carry a weight <math id="Sx1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge structure. In fact these can be assembled to give the prototype of what is called a <em id="Sx1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">variation of weight <math id="Sx1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge structure</em> (cf. for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">C-S-P</a>]</cite>).
So it is natural to look at <math id="Sx1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Sx1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a local system. The replacement for <math id="Sx1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is intersection cohomology <math id="Sx1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is an intrinsic way to relate this to ordinary cohomology. Indeed, the adjunction morphism gives a canonical map <math id="Sx1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> (see Remark <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> for details). As is the case for <math id="Sx1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, one assumes that <math id="Sx1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> carries a variation of Hodge structure. An extra technical assumption on <math id="Sx1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> has to be made which is known to hold<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          Because this system is defined over <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schm</a>, Lemma 4.5]</cite>.</span></span></span> for <math id="Sx1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{p}f_{*}\underline{{\mathbb{Q}}}_{Y}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>: we say that <math id="Sx1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Sx1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quasi-unipotent at infinity with respect to <math id="Sx1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></em> if for some (or any) embedded resolution <math id="Sx1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,X-U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,X-U)</annotation></semantics></math> the local monodromy operators around the branches of <math id="Sx1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}-U</annotation></semantics></math> are quasi-unipotent. Indeed, if <math id="Sx1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-unipotent at infinity
with respect to <math id="Sx1.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> we have canonical (pure<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          its weight is <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+n</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight of the variation of Hodge structure on <math id="footnote3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>, respectively mixed) Hodge structures on <math id="Sx1.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="Sx1.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the mixed Hodge structure on <math id="Sx1.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,V)</annotation></semantics></math> depends
only the projective bimeromorphic equivalence class of <math id="Sx1.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. This will be recalled in § <a href="#S3" title="3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. See in particular Coroll. <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 3.5. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To motivate the statement of the main theorem below, recall Zucker’s construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zuc</a>]</cite> for <math id="Sx1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Sx1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be the embedding of <math id="Sx1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> into its compactification. The sheaf <math id="Sx1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-isomorphic to the complex of holomorphic forms with values in <math id="Sx1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and with <math id="Sx1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> growth conditions at the boundary (with respect to the Poincaré metric). Forgetting the growth conditions gives a complex which computes the cohomology of <math id="Sx1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Sx1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>; whence a natural restriction map <math id="Sx1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The source is nothing but another incarnation of <math id="Sx1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> (Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm6" title="Remark 3.6. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>) and indeed, one of the main results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zuc</a>]</cite> states that it has a pure Hodge structure; moreover, it maps to the lowest weight part of the (special case of our) mixed Hodge structure on the target. 
Hence, in this setting, the lowest weight “comes from the compactification”.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this note concerns a generalization of the lowest weight property containing both Zucker’s result and the constant coefficients case as special cases:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthmx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthmx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmthmx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthmx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthmx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth complex manifold, <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> an analytic-Zariski open inclusion into a compact Kähler space and let <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a local system on <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, quasi-unipotent at infinity with respect to <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and carrying a polarizable variation of Hodge structure. Then

<br class="ltx_break"></span>a)<span id="Thmthmx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the natural morphism</span></p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right"><math id="Sx1.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthmx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthmx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>b)<span id="Thmthmx1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the image of the map in a) is exactly the lowest weight part of <math id="Thmthmx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> and is the same for Kähler compactifications which are projective-bimeromorphically equivalent.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let us make some comments on the statement of the theorem and its proof. Note that <math id="Sx1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not assumed to be a <em id="Sx1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smooth</em> compactification and that <math id="Sx1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-U</annotation></semantics></math> need not be a divisor with normal crossings. A condition like <math id="Sx1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> being Kähler is however unavoidable. As to the proof, a first point that needs to be shown is that the natural map (*) preserves Hodge and weight filtrations. The second point is that the image of this map, which then lands into the lowest weight part, is exactly the lowest weight part. Finally, since as we have seen, the mixed Hodge structure on <math id="Sx1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the compactification, one would like to show the lowest weight part depends only on the bimeromorphic equivalence class, as in the case of constant coefficients.
We can show this only for projective-bimeromorphically equivalent compactifications. The reason is that the decomposition theorem in the analytic setting at the moment is only available for projective morphisms (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>, 5.3.1]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The argument is not too hard if the complement is a hypersurface. This case is treated in § <a href="#S3" title="3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> together with the algebraic case. The general case can be reduced to it by blowing up. This yields the mixed Hodge structure on <math id="Sx1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>. To see how it relates to the Hodge structure on <math id="Sx1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> one needs a theory of mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces developed in § <a href="#S4" title="4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and one must study the direct image of the mixed Hodge complex under the blowing-down map. This is done in § <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> where the final step of the proof is given.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In order to make the proof as self-contained as possible, we start with a brief summary of the necessary results from the theory of perverse sheaves and mixed Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We want to make several side remarks for the algebraic case. The proof we give uses an argument which resembles the one from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha-Sa</a>, Remarks 2.2. i)]</cite> used in the <math id="Sx1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-adic situation and for constant coefficients (actually this works as long as the formalism of mixed sheaves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa91</a>]</cite> is satisfied).
Note also that our main theorem in the algebraic case does not follow from the mixed Hodge version of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor</a>, 3.1.4]</cite> unless <math id="Sx1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine morphism since the <math id="Sx1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-structure in loc. cit. is <span id="Sx1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> associated to the mixed complexes of weight <math id="Sx1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> in the usual sense, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor</a>, 3.1.2]</cite> (and Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm9" title="Remark 3.9. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> below).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The first named author wants to thank Stefan Müller-Stach for asking this question and urging him to write down a proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Perverse sheaves</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We only give a minimal exposition of the theory of perverse sheaves to explain the properties which will be used below. We shall only be working with the so-called middle perversity which respects Poincaré duality. Full details can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">B-B-D</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex analytic space. The category of perverse “sheaves” of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces on <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, is an <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">abelian category</em>. The fact that it is abelian follows from its very construction as a core with respect to a <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-structure. While the details of this are not so relevant for what follows, one needs to know that the starting point is formed by the <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constructible sheaves of <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces</em> on <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition these are sheaves of finite dimensional <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces which are locally constant on the strata of some analytic stratification of <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that the stratification is <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic</span> in the algebraic case. The simplest examples of such sheaves are the locally constant sheaves on <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> itself, or those which are locally constant on some locally Zariski closed subset <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> but zero elsewhere.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A core is defined with respect to a so-called <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-structure and in the perverse situation the <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-structure is defined by certain cohomological conditions, the so called support and co-support conditions. Indeed, instead of starting from complexes of constructible sheaves on <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> one departs from</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{cl}D_{c}^{\rm b}(X;{\mathbb{Q}}):&amp;\text{the derived category of bounded complexes}\\
&amp;\text{of sheaves of ${\mathbb{Q}}$-vector spaces on $X$ with}\\
&amp;\text{constructible cohomology sheaves.}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>the derived category of bounded complexes</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>of sheaves of </mtext><mi>ℚ</mi><mtext>-vector spaces on </mtext><mi>X</mi><mtext> with</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>constructible cohomology sheaves.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{cl}D_{c}^{\rm b}(X;{\mathbb{Q}}):&amp;\text{the derived category of bounded complexes}\\
&amp;\text{of sheaves of ${\mathbb{Q}}$-vector spaces on $X$ with}\\
&amp;\text{constructible cohomology sheaves.}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">By definition a perverse sheaf is such a complex <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> which obeys the support and co-support conditions:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathop{\rm supp}H^{p}(F)\leq-p,\quad\dim\mathop{\rm supp}H^{p}({\mathbb{D}}F)\leq-p," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">dim</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false">supp</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">dim</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false">supp</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathop{\rm supp}H^{p}(F)\leq-p,\quad\dim\mathop{\rm supp}H^{p}({\mathbb{D}}F)\leq-p,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}F:={\mathbb{R}}\underline{\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits}(F,{\mathbb{D}}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>Hom</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}F:={\mathbb{R}}\underline{\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits}(F,{\mathbb{D}}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is the Verdier dual of <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dualizing complex. For <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> smooth and <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, the case we shall be interested in, <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is  just <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}_{X}(d)[2d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}_{X}(d)[2d]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The support condition implies that <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}(F)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}(F)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> while the co-support condition implies <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p}(F)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p}(F)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;-d</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\dim X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\dim X</annotation></semantics></math>): perverse sheaves are complexes “concentrated in degrees between <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On a complex manifold a (finite rank) local system of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be made perverse by placing it in degree <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-d</annotation></semantics></math>: the complex <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math> is a perverse sheaf. If <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer smooth this complex has to be replaced by the so-called intersection complex. Indeed, if <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense Zariski-open subset of <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which consists of smooth points and <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is any (finite rank) local system of <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces on <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intersection complex</em> <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          Some people write <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> can be constructed as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">B-B-D</a>]</cite> (and <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([, Chap.V, 4]). ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> below).
(It is also called the minimal extension.)
By definition, its hypercohomology groups are the intersection cohomology groups:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}}[d]):={\mathbb{H}}^{k}(\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d]))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}}[d]):={\mathbb{H}}^{k}(\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmrmqx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmq">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrmqx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmrmqx1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrmqx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrmqx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even if <math id="Thmrmqx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> itself is smooth an intersection complex on <math id="Thmrmqx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> need not be of the form <math id="Thmrmqx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math> for some local system <math id="Thmrmqx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="Thmrmqx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> because of non-trivial monodromy “around infinity” <math id="Thmrmqx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following two results explain the role of these intersection complexes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bor84</a>, Chap.V, 4]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional irreducible complex analytic space and let <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth dense Zariski-open subset of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on which there is a local system <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite dimensional <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> vector spaces. The intersection complex <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math> is up to an isomorphism in the derived category the unique complex of sheaves of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces on <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which is perverse on <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, which restricts over <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math> and which has no non-trivial perverse sub or quotient objects supported on <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the situation of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([, Chap.V, 4]). ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, let <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be the inclusion and let <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math>. The adjunction morphism <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\#}:I\to j_{*}j^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>#</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\#}:I\to j_{*}j^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> induces a homomorphism</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}j^{\#}:IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}j^{\#}:IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which will be used to compare intersection and ordinary cohomology.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">B-B-D</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact or algebraic, <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Artinian and Noetherian. Its simple objects are the intersection complexes <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{Z}({\mathbb{V}}[\dim Z])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{Z}({\mathbb{V}}[\dim Z])</annotation></semantics></math> supported on an irreducible subspace <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> and where <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated to an irreducible representation of <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset Z</annotation></semantics></math> the largest open subset of <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> over which <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is locally
constant.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We also need <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">filtered</em> objects in the abelian category <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. A priori these are not represented by filtered complexes in the usual sense of the word, since the morphisms are in a derived category: they are “fractions” <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[f]/[s]:K\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[f]/[s]:K\to L</annotation></semantics></math> where the bracket stands for the corresponding homotopy class, <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:K\to N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:K\to N</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of complexes and <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;s\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\leftarrowfill\crcr}}\;}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtable align="bottom" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;s\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\leftarrowfill\crcr}}\;}L</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism. However, the category of sheaves on <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with constructible cohomology has enough injectives and replacing <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by a complex <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of injective objects, the quasi-isomorphism <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> becomes invertible up to homotopy and so <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[f]/[s]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[f]/[s]</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a true morphism <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\to L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\to L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, recall:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any morphism of complexes <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:A\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:A\to B</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism in the derived category defined by it can be represented by an injective morphism <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\to B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\to B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of complexes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof</span>:   Take <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B^{\prime}:=\mathop{\rm Cone}(-\mathop{\rm id}\nolimits\oplus v:A\to A\oplus B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Cone</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">id</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}:=\mathop{\rm Cone}(-\mathop{\rm id}\nolimits\oplus v:A\to A\oplus B)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a subcomplex of <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and we get an injective morphism <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\to B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\to B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is identified with <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> by the quasi-isomorphism <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(0,v,\mathop{\rm id}\nolimits):B^{\prime}\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">id</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,v,\mathop{\rm id}\nolimits):B^{\prime}\to B</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Any <em id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">finite</em> filtration on <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a filtered complex in <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof</span>:   Induction on the length of the filtration, assumed to be an increasing filtration <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. The above discussion shows that the morphism <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}\to W_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}\to W_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a morphism of complexes to which Lemma. <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 1.4. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> can be applied.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Mixed Hodge Modules</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we put together some properties of mixed Hodge modules which will be used in the sequel.
These properties are proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>]</cite>. See also the exposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">P-S</a>, Cha. 14]</cite> where mixed Hodge Modules are introduced axiomatically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex algebraic variety or a complex analytic space. There exists an abelian category <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{MHM}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{MHM}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, the category of <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed Hodge modules</em> on <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmrmqx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmq">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrmqx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmrmqx2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrmqx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrmqx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for nonproper complex algebraic varieties <math id="Thmrmqx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> we always have <math id="Thmrmqx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{MHM}}(X)\neq{\mathsf{MHM}}(X^{\rm an})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{MHM}}(X)\neq{\mathsf{MHM}}(X^{\rm an})</annotation></semantics></math> because of the difference between algebraic and analytic stratifications. Note also that a mixed Hodge module on an algebraic variety is always <em id="Thmrmqx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">assumed</em> to be extendable under an open immersion. The last property cannot be well-formulated in the analytic case due to the defect of the Zariski topology on analytic spaces, e.g. Zariski-open immersions are not stable by composition and closed subspaces are not intersections of hypersurfaces Zariski-locally.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proprs">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Properties 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">A)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a functor</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{\rm rat}_{X}:D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)\to D^{\rm b}_{c}(X;{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>rat</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{\rm rat}_{X}:D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)\to D^{\rm b}_{c}(X;{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is sent to <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Perv</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Perv}\nolimits(X;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. One says that <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{\rm rat}_{X}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>rat</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{\rm rat}_{X}M</annotation></semantics></math> is the underlying
rational perverse sheaf of <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we say that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M\in\mathsf{MHM}(X)\mbox{ \emph{is supported on }}Z\iff\mbox{\rm rat}_{X}M\,\mbox{is
supported on }Z." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is supported on </em></mtext></mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><msub><mtext>rat</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>is
supported on </mtext><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathsf{MHM}(X)\mbox{ \emph{is supported on }}Z\iff\mbox{\rm rat}_{X}M\,\mbox{is
supported on }Z.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">B)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The category of mixed Hodge modules supported on a point is the category
of graded polarizable rational mixed Hodge structures; the functor “<span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">rat</span>” associates to the mixed
Hodge structure the underlying rational vector space.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">C)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each object <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{MHM}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{MHM}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <em id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weight filtration</em> <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">morphisms preserve the weight filtration strictly;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the object <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M</annotation></semantics></math> is semisimple in <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a point the <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration is the usual weight filtration for the mixed
Hodge structure.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian category, the cohomology groups of any complex of mixed
Hodge modules on <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are again mixed Hodge modules on <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. With this in mind, we say that
for a complex <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weight</em> satisfies</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{weight}[M]\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\leq n,\\
\geq n\end{array}\right.\iff\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits_{k}^{W}H^{i}(M)=0\,\,\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\mbox{for }k&gt;i+n\\
\mbox{for }k&lt;i+n.\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>weight</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{weight}[M]\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\leq n,\\
\geq n\end{array}\right.\iff\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits_{k}^{W}H^{i}(M)=0\,\,\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\mbox{for }k&gt;i+n\\
\mbox{for }k&lt;i+n.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We observe that if we consider the weight filtration on the mixed Hodge modules which constitute a complex <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of mixed Hodge modules we get a filtered complex in this category.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">D)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(i) For each morphism <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> between <em id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex algebraic varieties</em>, there are induced functors <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{*},f_{!}:D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)\to D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*},f_{!}:D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)\to D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{*},f^{!}:D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(Y)\to D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*},f^{!}:D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(Y)\to D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> which lift the functors <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{!}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{-1},f^{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1},f^{!}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively; the latter two functors are defined on the level of complexes of sheaves on <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> (whose cohomology is constructible.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">D)(ii) In the <em id="S2.I1.i4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">analytic case</em> (i) is no longer necessarily true but we have:

<br class="ltx_break">— for <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> projective or if <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact Kähler and <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\text{\rm pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\text{\rm pt}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are <em id="S2.I1.i4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cohomological</em> functors <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}f_{*}=H^{i}f_{!}:\mathsf{MHM}(X)\to\mathsf{MHM}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f_{*}=H^{i}f_{!}:\mathsf{MHM}(X)\to\mathsf{MHM}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> which lift the perverse cohomological functor <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{p}R^{i}f_{*}={}^{p}R^{i}f_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}R^{i}f_{*}={}^{p}R^{i}f_{!}</annotation></semantics></math>;

<br class="ltx_break">— for any <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> there are cohomological functors <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}f^{*},H^{i}f^{!}:\mathsf{MHM}(Y)\to\mathsf{MHM}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f^{*},H^{i}f^{!}:\mathsf{MHM}(Y)\to\mathsf{MHM}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> which lift <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}H^{i}f^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}H^{i}f^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I1.i4.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{p}H^{i}f^{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}H^{i}f^{!}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">E)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The functors <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{*},f_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*},f_{!}</annotation></semantics></math> do not increase weights in the sense that if <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
has weights <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, the same is true for <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{!}M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">F)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The functors <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{*},f^{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*},f^{!}</annotation></semantics></math> do not decrease weights in the sense that if <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has
weights <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>, the same is true for <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{!}M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">G)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, <math id="S2.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves weights, i.e. <math id="S2.I1.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> neither increases nor decreases weights.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmks">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remarks 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) Despite the fact that the functors <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> etc. do not exist in the analytic setting, properties E), F), G) still have a meaning as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.26]</cite> since the weight is defined in terms of cohomology only.

<br class="ltx_break">2) Since in the analytic setting Zariski-open immersions are not stable by composition <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}f_{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f_{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}f_{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f_{!}M</annotation></semantics></math> do not necessarily exist for analytic morphisms <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. This explains why in the analytic case D) not all morphisms are allowed.

<br class="ltx_break">3) The reader may interpret the Kähler condition on <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in Property D) as the existence of a projective morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> from a Kähler <em id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">manifold</em> <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the construction of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}f_{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f_{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to</annotation></semantics></math>pt , where <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a pure Hodge module, is reduced to the assertion for <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>: use the decomposition theorem for <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> applied to a pure Hodge module on <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a subquotient of the pullback of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">C-K-S</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">K-K86</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">K-K87</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">K-K89</a>]</cite>. For the mixed case we can use the weight spectral sequence.

<br class="ltx_break">4) It is still unclear whether <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}f_{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f_{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> exists for proper Kähler morphisms <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is constant, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90b</a>]</cite>. If the reader prefers, he may assume that the polarizable Hodge modules in this paper are direct factors of the cohomological direct images of the constant sheaf by smooth Kähler morphisms so that the existence of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}f_{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}f_{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the decomposition theorem for the direct image of the constant sheaf by proper Kähler morphisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90b</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above properties readily imply various basic properties of mixed Hodge modules. For example,
if <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex of mixed Hodge modules on <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> its cohomology <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{q}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{q}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed Hodge module on <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Properties B) and D) imply:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{X}:X\to\mbox{\rm pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{X}:X\to\mbox{\rm pt}</annotation></semantics></math> be the constant map to the point. Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraic or compact Kähler. Then for any complex <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of mixed Hodge modules on <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{p}(X,M):=H^{p}((a_{X})_{*}M)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{p}(X,M):=H^{p}((a_{X})_{*}M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a mixed Hodge structure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of the main theorem one needs the following two technical constructions. The first is the adjunction construction:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_constr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic varieties</span> and a mixed Hodge module <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The adjunction morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\#}:M\to f_{*}f^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>#</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\#}:M\to f_{*}f^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of complexes of mixed Hodge modules. For any bounded complex <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of mixed Hodge modules on <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{X}=a_{Y}\raise 1.0pt\hbox{{$\scriptscriptstyle\circ$}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.500em">∘</mo></mpadded><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{X}=a_{Y}\raise 1.0pt\hbox{{$\scriptscriptstyle\circ$}}f</annotation></semantics></math> induces a canonical identification <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{n}(Y,f_{*}K)={\mathbb{H}}^{n}(X,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{n}(Y,f_{*}K)={\mathbb{H}}^{n}(X,K)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular this holds for <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=f^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=f^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>. Adjunction thus induces a morphism of mixed Hodge structures</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}f^{\#}:{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(Y,M)\to{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(X,f^{*}M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}f^{\#}:{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(Y,M)\to{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(X,f^{*}M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">analytic case</em> this construction remains valid for an open immersion <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> whose complement is a hypersurface (defined locally by a function <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>). Indeed, then <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}j^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}j^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed Hodge module whose underlying <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-module comes from localization by <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the underlying <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-module of <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, consider the complement <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of an intersection <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of global hypersurfaces. Then <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}j^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}j^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex of mixed Hodge modules due to a second construction:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_constr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.19, 2.20]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,r</annotation></semantics></math> be holomorphic functions on <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}g_{i}^{-1}(0)_{\rm red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>red</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\bigcap_{i=1}^{r}g_{i}^{-1}(0)_{\rm red}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=Y-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=Y-Z</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=Y-g_{i}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=Y-g_{i}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset\{1,\dots,r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset\{1,\dots,r\}</annotation></semantics></math> we set <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{I}=\bigcap_{i\in I}Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{I}=\bigcap_{i\in I}Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Z\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Z\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{I}:Y_{I}\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{I}:Y_{I}\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the natural inclusions. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a mixed Hodge module on <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>; then also <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, is a mixed Hodge module on <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and there are quasi-isomorphisms in the category <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}\mathsf{MHM}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.EGx1.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
