<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle i_{*}i^{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i_{*}i^{!}M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\sim\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable align="bottom" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\sim\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle[\cdots 0\to M\to B_{1}\to B_{2}\cdots B_{r}\to 0],\quad B_{k}=\bigoplus_{|I|=k}(j_{I})_{*}j^{*}_{I}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\cdots 0\to M\to B_{1}\to B_{2}\cdots B_{r}\to 0],\quad B_{k}=\bigoplus_{|I|=k}(j_{I})_{*}j^{*}_{I}M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.EGx1.2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j_{*}j^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j_{*}j^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\sim\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable align="bottom" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\sim\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle[\cdots 0\to B_{1}\to B_{2}\to B_{3}\cdots\to B_{r}\to 0],\quad\text{ $B_{k}$ in degree $k-1$. }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mtext> in degree </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\cdots 0\to B_{1}\to B_{2}\to B_{3}\cdots\to B_{r}\to 0],\quad\text{ $B_{k}$ in degree $k-1$. }</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The above construction leads to</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>( <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, (4.4.1)]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Z\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Z\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed immersion and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U=Y-Z\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U=Y-Z\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the inclusion of the complement. Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y,Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y,Z</annotation></semantics></math> are algebraic or, alternatively, that <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is an intersection of global hypersurfaces of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a
mixed Hodge module on <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a distinguished triangle <span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">5</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">We shall write triangles also as </span><math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}\to M\to M^{\prime\prime}\to[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}\to M\to M^{\prime\prime}\to[1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span></span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hskip 60.00009pt\begin{matrix}i_{*}i^{!}M&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\hskip 8.19447pt\hskip 8.19447pt\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;M\\
\vskip 12.91663pt\\
&amp;j_{*}j^{*}M\\
\end{matrix}\begin{picture}(0.0,0.0)\put(-20.0,-4.0){\vector{-3}{4}{5.0}}\put(-5.0,5.0){\vector{-3}{-4}{5.0}}\put(-7.0,0.0){$\scriptstyle\alpha$}\put(-22.0,0.0){$\scriptstyle[1]$}\end{picture}\hskip-60.00009pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mtable align="bottom" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>→</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd><mtd><mi>M</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>         <math alttext="\scriptstyle\alpha" display="inline"><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi></math>   <math alttext="\scriptstyle[1]" display="inline"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></math>   </mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hskip 60.00009pt\begin{matrix}i_{*}i^{!}M&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\hskip 8.19447pt\hskip 8.19447pt\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;M\\
\vskip 12.91663pt\\
&amp;j_{*}j^{*}M\\
\end{matrix}\begin{picture}(0.0,0.0)\put(-20.0,-4.0){\vector{-3}{4}{5.0}}\put(-5.0,5.0){\vector{-3}{-4}{5.0}}\put(-7.0,0.0){$\scriptstyle\alpha$}\put(-22.0,0.0){$\scriptstyle[1]$}\end{picture}\hskip-60.00009pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in the bounded derived category of mixed Hodge modules lifting the analogous triangle for complexes with constructible cohomology sheaves. The morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> induces
the adjunction morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}j^{\#}:{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(Y,M)\to{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(U,j^{*}M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}j^{\#}:{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(Y,M)\to{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(U,j^{*}M)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> (see (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In Construction 2.4. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof</span>:   In the algebraic setting the local constructions <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Construction 2.5 ([, 2.19, 2.20]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> for a suitable affine cover patch together to give globally defined quasi-isomorphisms for <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{*}i^{!}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}i^{!}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}j^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}j^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>. The local construction shows the existence of the distinguished triangle. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 4.4.1]</cite> for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The same argument applies in the analytic case under the assumption that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a global complete intersection. See the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.19]</cite>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reader may wonder what happens in the general setting of analytic spaces. The problem is that Construction <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Construction 2.5 ([, 2.19, 2.20]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> can not be globalized to complexes of mixed Hodge modules. However, the <em id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cohomology sheaves</em> of the complexes do make sense globally and are indeed mixed Hodge modules. Hence also the long exact sequence in cohomology associated to (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In Lemma 2.6 ( [, (4.4.1)]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) exists in the category of mixed Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">irreducible</em> compact Kähler analytic space of dimension <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be the inclusion of a dense Zariski-open subset for which we make the crucial assumption that <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is smooth.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We shall not review here the definitions and properties of polarizable Hodge modules.
For our purposes we only need the following basic result linking variations of Hodge structures and polarizable Hodge modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>, Th. 5.4.3]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polarizable variation of Hodge structure on <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, there is a polarizable Hodge module <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> whose underlying perverse component is <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There is however, one important aside to make at this point.
In the algebraic setting mixed Hodge modules are assumed to extend under open immersions, but this ceases to hold in the analytic category. Instead, one replaces it by the following condition on the underlying local system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a local system on <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quasi-unipotent at infinity with respect to <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></em>, if for some (or any) choice of an embedded resolution <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,X-U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,X-U)</annotation></semantics></math>
the local monodromy operators of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}-U</annotation></semantics></math> are quasi-unipotent.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrmqx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmq">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrmqx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmrmqx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrmqx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrmqx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ksh</a>]</cite> this property is independent of the choice of <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and depends only on the bimeromorphic equivalence class of <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a good compactification of <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> together with a bimeromorphic map <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>; these exist: by blowing up <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in suitable ideals on can even assume that <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is projective. So one can test quasi-unipotency on such <math id="Thmrmqx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a polarizable Hodge module this notion leads to <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pure</em> Hodge modules (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>]</cite>).
In both settings (polarizable algebraic Hodge modules and pure mixed Hodge modules in the analytic case) one obtains semi-simple categories: this is implied by the polarizability condition, see also <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.C). Both categories satisfy moreover the strict support condition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_propr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A polarizable weight <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge module <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of polarizable weight <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> Hodge modules <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> which have strict support<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is said to have <em id="footnote6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strict support <math id="footnote6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math></em> if it is supported on <math id="footnote6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> but no quotient or sub object of <math id="footnote6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has support on a proper subvariety of <math id="footnote6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible subvarieties of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and the same assertion holds for pure Hodge modules.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 3.20, 3.21]</cite> one has:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and that <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-unipotent at infinity with respect to <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and underlies a polarized variation of Hodge structures of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a unique pure Hodge module <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> having strict support in <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and which restricts over <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrmqx4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmq">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrmqx4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmrmqx4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrmqx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrmqx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that this checks with the assertion in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([, Chap.V, 4]). ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> which holds for the rational component of the mixed Hodge modules.

More precisely: the intersection complex <math id="Thmrmqx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math> is the rational component of <math id="Thmrmqx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This remark is crucial for the proof of the next corollary.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1)<span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> There exists a mixed Hodge structure on <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>. It depends only on the projective bimeromorphic equivalence class of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>2)<span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> carries a pure Hodge structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof</span>:   1) Replacing <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by a suitable blow up, we may assume that <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a good compactification of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Construction 2.5 ([, 2.19, 2.20]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}j^{*}V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}j^{*}V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> then is a mixed Hodge module on <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> the cohomology group <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> carries the mixed Hodge structure <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d}(j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d}(j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})</annotation></semantics></math>. If there are two good compactifications <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with a projective morphism <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X_{1}\to X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X_{1}\to X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> inducing
an isomorphism over <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, then with <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{k}:U\hookrightarrow X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{k}:U\hookrightarrow X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> the embeddings, we have an isomorphism of mixed Hodge modules <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\pi_{*}Rj_{1*}V_{X_{1}}^{\rm Hdg}=Rj_{2*}V_{X_{2}}^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\pi_{*}Rj_{1*}V_{X_{1}}^{\rm Hdg}=Rj_{2*}V_{X_{2}}^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> by the uniqueness of <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rj_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rj_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.11]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break">2) Since <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pure Hodge module, by the previous Remark and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> <math id="S3.p6.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{k}(X,\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}})=IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}})=IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> carries the mixed Hodge structure <math id="S3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k-d}(X,V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k-d}(X,V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})</annotation></semantics></math>, which by Properties <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> G) is pure of weight <math id="S3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+n</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that in addition <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-U</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor with normal crossings. Then, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">C-K-S</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">K-K86</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">K-K87</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">K-K89</a>]</cite> <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> provided one measures integrability with respect to the Poincaré metric around infinity (one is in the normal crossing situation, so locally around infinity one has a product of disks and punctured disks).
Summarizing:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}))=IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})=L^{2}H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{k}(\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}))=IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})=L^{2}H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">has a pure Hodge structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next one wants to relate intersection cohomology and ordinary cohomology. This is the content of the main theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, that <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-unipotent at infinity with respect to <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and that it carries a polarized variation of Hodge structure of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then

<br class="ltx_break"></span>a)<span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The natural morphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}j^{\#}:IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}j^{\#}:IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(see (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)) is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures;

<br class="ltx_break"></span>b)<span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the image of <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}j^{\#}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}j^{\#}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the lowest weight part of <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> and this image is the same for Kähler compactifications which are projective-bimeromorphically equivalent to <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof in the algebraic case.</span> 
Let <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Z=X-U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Z=X-U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be the inclusion. Set <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}=j_{*}j^{*}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}=j_{*}j^{*}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M^{\prime\prime}=i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime\prime}=i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formula (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In Construction 2.4. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) for the inclusion <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> and the mixed Hodge module <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> shows that (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is indeed a morphism of mixed Hodge structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Form the distinguished triangle (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In Lemma 2.6 ( [, (4.4.1)]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Portion of its associated long exact sequence in hypercohomology reads</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hskip-5.0pt\begin{matrix}\cdots\to IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;H^{k}j^{\#}\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})&amp;&amp;\\
\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to8.61108pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}&amp;&amp;\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to8.61108pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}&amp;&amp;\\
{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d}(X,M)&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\phantom{H^{k}j^{\#}}\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d}(X,M^{\prime})&amp;\to&{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d+1}(X,M^{\prime\prime})\to\dots\end{matrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mtable align="bottom" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mi mathsize="0.700em">H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo>‖</mo><mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo>‖</mo><mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mtable align="bottom" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hskip-5.0pt\begin{matrix}\cdots\to IH^{k}(X,{\mathbb{V}})&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;H^{k}j^{\#}\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})&amp;&amp;\\
\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to8.61108pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}&amp;&amp;\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to8.61108pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}&amp;&amp;\\
{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d}(X,M)&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;\phantom{H^{k}j^{\#}}\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d}(X,M^{\prime})&amp;\to&amp;{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d+1}(X,M^{\prime\prime})\to\dots\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+d</annotation></semantics></math>, and so by Property <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.F the complex <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> has weight <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq n+d</annotation></semantics></math>. By Property <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.G this also holds for the complex <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M^{\prime\prime}=i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime\prime}=i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying once more Property <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.G to the functor <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{X})_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{X})_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> one sees that <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d+1}(X,M^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{k-d+1}(X,M^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> has weights <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq k+n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq k+n+1</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the image of the map (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is exactly the weight <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+n)</annotation></semantics></math>-part of <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(U,{\mathbb{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In the algebraic category the last assertion of b) can be replaced by a stronger assertion: we may assume that two compactifications are related by a proper algebraic morphism to which the decomposition theorem can be applied. Instead of giving full details here we refer to the proof in the analytic setting which is given at the end of § <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and which is similar in spirit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Strategy of the proof in the analytic setting.</span>
If <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a hypersurface, the same proof works in view of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 2.6 ( [, (4.4.1)]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>. This Lemma also shows that (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In the general situation one has to perform a suitable blow-up <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> which is the identity in <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}=X^{\prime}-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}=X^{\prime}-U</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor. Now we would like to apply the functor <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The problem is that this functor does not exist in the derived categories of mixed Hodge modules. So we have to find a substitute for this which still preserves enough of the Properties  <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> so that we can complete the proof as in the algebraic case. It turns out that the correct category to use is the one of mixed Hodge complexes. See § <a href="#S4" title="4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. In § <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we complete the proof in the analytic case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the algebraic setting the following claim is easily shown to imply the main result as well and can be seen as a refinement of it.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaimx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaimx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Claim</span></span><span id="Thmclaimx1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaimx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaimx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="Thmclaimx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally principal divisor or <math id="Thmclaimx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine morphism. Then the adjunction morphism <math id="Thmclaimx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\#}:V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}\to j_{*}j^{*}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>#</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\#}:V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}\to j_{*}j^{*}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective and identifies <math id="Thmclaimx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> with the lowest weight part of <math id="Thmclaimx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}j^{*}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}j^{*}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, the extra hypothesis on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> implies
(see Construction <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Construction 2.5 ([, 2.19, 2.20]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.11]</cite>)
that <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed Hodge module (not just a <em id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex</em> of mixed Hodge modules) and the main theorem then follows easily from the Claim. The latter follows from the long exact sequence <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\to H^{0}i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}\to V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}\to j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}\to H^{1}i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to H^{0}i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}\to V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}\to j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}\to H^{1}i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> using that the strict support condition implies that <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{0}i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}i_{*}i^{!}V^{\rm Hdg}_{X}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above claim can alternatively be shown using adjunction. This was how the first named author originally proved the main result<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            See <span id="footnote7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">http://arxiv.org/abs/0708.0130v2</span> </span></span></span>. Here is the argument. It suffices to show that the lowest weight part <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d+n}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d+n}M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> has no quotient or sub object supported on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=X-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=X-U</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a pure weight mixed Hodge module, and hence, by Property <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.C a semi-simple object in the category of mixed Hodge modules. By construction, it restricts to <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. By semi-simplicity a quotient object is also a sub object and hence it suffices to show that there are no mixed Hodge modules <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> of pure weight <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+d</annotation></semantics></math> supported on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,W_{n+d}M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>Hom</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,W_{n+d}M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> in the abelian category <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of mixed Hodge modules. Functoriality of the weight filtration implies
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,W_{n+d}M)=\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>Hom</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hom</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,W_{n+d}M)=\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(A)</annotation></semantics></math> be the derived category of bounded complexes in <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the natural map <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,M)\to\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(N,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>Hom</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hom</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{A}(N,M)\to\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(N,M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a bijection (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Verd77</a>, p. 293]</cite>) it is enough to show that <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(N,M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>Hom</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(N,M)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In the derived category one can use the adjunction for <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j^{*},j_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j^{*},j_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> yielding <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(N,j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})=\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(j^{*}N,V^{\rm Hdg})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>Hom</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Hom</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(N,j_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})=\mathop{\rm Hom}\nolimits_{D(A)}(j^{*}N,V^{\rm Hdg})=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{*}N=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}N=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It would not be difficult to construct a mixed Hodge version of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor</a>, 3.1.4]</cite>. However, this would not immediately imply our main theorem unless <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine morphism. Indeed, the <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-structure in loc. cit. is defined by the condition that <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{p}H^{i}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{p}H^{i}K</annotation></semantics></math> has weight <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> and not <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq i+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq i+k</annotation></semantics></math> as in the case of mixed Hodge complexes of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor</a>, 3.1.2]</cite>. It does not seem that there exists a <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-structure associated to mixed complexes of weight <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq k</annotation></semantics></math> since the weight filtration is not strict and the weight spectral sequence does not degenerate at <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see also Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> on the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> in the analytic case where mixed Hodge complexes in the Hodge setting are used).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> in the analytic case we need a theory of mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces which refines Deligne’s theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del71</a>]</cite> of cohomological mixed Hodge complexes. We present it here in a rather simplified manner which has the defect that the mapping cones are not well-defined. However, this does not cause a problem for the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> since all we need is the existence of the long exact sequence (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa00</a>]</cite> for a more elaborate formulation taking care of the problem with the cones.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmnotax1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_nota">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmnotax1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation</span></span><span id="Thmnotax1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmnotax1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmnotax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">— <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MFW(D_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MFW(D_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>: the category of filtered <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,F)</annotation></semantics></math> with a finite filtration <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. For singular <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> this can be defined by using closed embeddings of open subsets of <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into complex manifolds, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>, 2.1.20]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break">— <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>: the derived category of bounded complexes <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,F,W)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
1) the sheaves <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{p}H^{i}F_{p}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{p}H^{i}F_{p}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M</annotation></semantics></math> are coherent over the sheaf <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{p}F_{p}D_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{p}F_{p}D_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
and 2) the sheaves <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M</annotation></semantics></math> are holonomic <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.

<br class="ltx_break">— <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>: the derived category of of bounded filtered complexes <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}K\in D^{\rm b}_{c}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}K\in D^{\rm b}_{c}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: we define <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.

<br class="ltx_break">— <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>: the “fibre” product of <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> where the functor <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm DR}:D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})\to D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>DR</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm DR}:D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})\to D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the de Rham functor is used to glue the two categories. More precisely, its objects are triples</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}=((M,F,W),(K,W),\alpha)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}=((M,F,W),(K,W),\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmnotax1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,F,W)\in D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,F,W)\in D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K,W)\in D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,W)\in D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\mathop{\rm DR}(M,W)\cong(K,W)\otimes_{{\mathbb{Q}}}{\mathbb{C}}\quad\hbox{in}\,\,D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">DR</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\mathop{\rm DR}(M,W)\cong(K,W)\otimes_{{\mathbb{Q}}}{\mathbb{C}}\quad\hbox{in}\,\,D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmnotax1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and morphisms in the category are pairs of morphisms of <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{c}W(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
Forgetting the filtration <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> we can define <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{c}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{c}(X,{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmnotax1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{\hskip-30.00005pt| \hskip 30.00005pt}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}=(\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}(M,F),\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}K,\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}\alpha)\in D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>— </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{\hskip-30.00005pt| \hskip 30.00005pt}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}=(\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}(M,F),\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}K,\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}\alpha)\in D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1) The category of mixed Hodge complexes <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHC}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHC}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the full subcategory of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}FW(D_{X},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}=((M,F,W),(K,W),\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}=((M,F,W),(K,W),\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following conditions for <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,i</annotation></semantics></math>:

<br class="ltx_break">(i) The <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits_{k}^{W}(M,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits_{k}^{W}(M,F)</annotation></semantics></math> are strict and we have a decomposition</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}\cong\bigoplus_{j}(H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}})[-j]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}\cong\bigoplus_{j}(H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}})[-j].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(ii) The <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> are polarizable Hodge modules of weight <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">2) Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category of <em id="S4.Thmthm1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weakly mixed Hodge modules</em>, i.e. its objects have a weight filtration <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> for which the gradeds <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are polarizable Hodge modules of weight <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, but there is no condition on the extension between the graded pieces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">3) We say that <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}\overset{u}{\to}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\overset{v}{\to}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\overset{w}{\to}{\mathcal{M}}[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑢</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑣</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑤</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}\overset{u}{\to}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\overset{v}{\to}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\overset{w}{\to}{\mathcal{M}}[1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weakly distinguished triangle</em> in <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHC}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHC}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v,w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v,w</annotation></semantics></math> are morphisms of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHC}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHC}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and its underlying triangle of complexes of sheaves of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces is distinguished. Here the weight filtration <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}[1]</annotation></semantics></math> is shifted by 1 so that <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}[1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a mixed Hodge complex.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrmqx5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmq">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrmqx5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmrmqx5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrmqx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrmqx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case <math id="Thmrmqx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=</annotation></semantics></math>pt , we do not have to assume the decomposition (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Definition 4.1. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) in condition (i) of Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>,1). One reason is that this is only needed to prove the stability by the direct image under a morphism from <math id="Thmrmqx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Another reason is that this decomposition actually follows from the other conditions in this case since the category of vector spaces over a field is semisimple.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>, 5.1.14]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The category <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian category whose morphisms are strictly compatible with <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a mixed Hodge complex <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}=(H^{i}(M,F),{}^{p}H^{i}(K),{}^{p}H^{i}\alpha)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}=(H^{i}(M,F),{}^{p}H^{i}(K),{}^{p}H^{i}\alpha).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We put a weight filtration on it by letting <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}W_{k-i}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}W_{k-i}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> (or, equivalently, the one induced by the filtration <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Dec}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">Dec</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Dec}W</annotation></semantics></math> for the underlying <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-module (cf. Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> below). This shift of the filtration <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> comes from condition (ii) in the above definition of <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHC}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHC}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>, 1.3.6 and 5.1.11]</cite>, etc. we have</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With the weight filtration <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> defined above, the <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> are weakly mixed Hodge modules.
There is a weight spectral sequence in the abelian category of weakly mixed Hodge modules <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{p,q}=H^{p+q}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{-p}{\mathcal{M}}\Rightarrow H^{p+q}{\mathcal{M}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{p,q}=H^{p+q}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{-p}{\mathcal{M}}\Rightarrow H^{p+q}{\mathcal{M}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which degenerates at <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and whose abutting filtration on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+q}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+q}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the weight filtration of weakly mixed Hodge modules shifted by <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q</annotation></semantics></math> as above, i.e.</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{p,q}=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{q}H^{p+q}{\mathcal{M}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{p,q}=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{q}H^{p+q}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,F,\mathop{\rm Dec}W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">Dec</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,F,\mathop{\rm Dec}W)</annotation></semantics></math> is bistrict, and the weight filtration on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p+q}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p+q}M</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Dec}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">Dec</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Dec}W</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the underlying <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-module of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathop{\rm Dec}W)_{k}M^{i}:=\mathop{\rm Ker}(d:W_{k-i}M^{i}\to\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k-i}M^{i+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">Dec</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">Ker</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathop{\rm Dec}W)_{k}M^{i}:=\mathop{\rm Ker}(d:W_{k-i}M^{i}\to\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k-i}M^{i+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> we get</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A weakly distinguished triangle as in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, 3) induces a long exact sequence in the abelian category <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}\overset{u}{\to}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\overset{v}{\to}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\overset{w}{\to}H^{i+1}{\mathcal{M}}\to." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑢</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑣</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑤</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}\overset{u}{\to}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\overset{v}{\to}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\overset{w}{\to}H^{i+1}{\mathcal{M}}\to.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For a morphism of mixed Hodge complexes <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathcal{M}}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathcal{M}}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mapping cone</span> <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}:=\mathop{\rm Cone}(u:{\mathcal{M}}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Cone</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}:=\mathop{\rm Cone}(u:{\mathcal{M}}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the usual way. Here the weight filtration <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}[1]</annotation></semantics></math> is shifted by 1 so that <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>W</mi></msup><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}u</annotation></semantics></math> in the graded pieces of the differential of <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes and hence conditions (i) and (ii) above are satisfied. However, <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not unique</span> up to a non-canonical isomorphism because of a problem of homotopy. So we cannot get a triangulated category although there is a weakly distinguished triangle <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to[1]</annotation></semantics></math>
which by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> induces the long exact sequence (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) in the category <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHW}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHW}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since the weight filtration on the perverse component of a weakly mixed Hodge module can be represented by an honest filtered complex (Cor. <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 1.5. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Considering a weakly mixed Hodge module as a mixed Hodge complex concentrated in degree <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> we get a functor</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{X}:\mathsf{MHW}(X)\to\mathsf{MHC}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{X}:\mathsf{MHW}(X)\to\mathsf{MHC}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism, and let <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a polarizable Hodge module. The object <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}(\iota_{X}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}(\iota_{X}(M))</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{Y},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{Y},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The decomposition theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa88</a>, 5.3.1]</cite> can be applied to <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and applying <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> to the resulting Hodge modules yields elements in <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{Y},{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{Y},{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The uniqueness of the decomposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del94</a>]</cite> then implies:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective morphism, and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of a polarizable Hodge module by <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have a decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}{\mathcal{M}}\cong\bigoplus_{i}(H^{i}f_{*}{\mathcal{M}})[-i]\quad\text{in}\,\,\,D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{Y},{\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">in</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}{\mathcal{M}}\cong\bigoplus_{i}(H^{i}f_{*}{\mathcal{M}})[-i]\quad\text{in}\,\,\,D^{\rm b}_{h}F(D_{Y},{\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this with Properties <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.D) (ii) we get</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mixed Hodge complexes and weakly distinguished triangles are stable by the direct image under <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is projective or if <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is compact Kähler and <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=pt</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrmqx6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmq">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrmqx6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmrmqx6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrmqx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrmqx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the stability by direct images asserted in Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 4.7. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> does not follow from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 4.6. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> if we replace <math id="Thmrmqx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+i</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmrmqx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in condition (ii) in the above definition of <math id="Thmrmqx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{MHC}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖧𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{MHC}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. (This causes the shift of the filtration <math id="Thmrmqx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> below.)</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> in the analytic case</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a bimeromorphic projective morphism inducing the identity over <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}-U</annotation></semantics></math> is a hypersurface (defined locally by a function). Let <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}:U\to X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}:U\to X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inclusion. Then <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}j^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}j^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math> is a perverse sheaf, and underlies a mixed Hodge module <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.17]</cite>. By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 4.5. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> this gives a mixed Hodge complex concentrated in degree <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S5.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}=((M^{\prime},F,W),(K^{\prime},W),\alpha):=\iota_{X^{\prime}}(j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}=((M^{\prime},F,W),(K^{\prime},W),\alpha):=\iota_{X^{\prime}}(j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}={\mathbb{R}}j^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}={\mathbb{R}}j^{\prime}_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote the direct image of <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}=((M,F,W),(K,W),\alpha):=\pi_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}=(\pi_{*}(M^{\prime},F,W),\pi_{*}(K^{\prime},W),\pi_{*}\alpha)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}=((M,F,W),(K,W),\alpha):=\pi_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}=(\pi_{*}(M^{\prime},F,W),\pi_{*}(K^{\prime},W),\pi_{*}\alpha).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 4.7. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> this is a mixed Hodge complex since <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}H^{0}{\mathcal{M}}=\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}H^{0}{\mathcal{M}}=\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=d+n+i,i\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=d+n+i,i\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> or if <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;d+n+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;d+n+i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof</span>:   It suffices to show the assertion for the underlying complex of <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> since the condition on strict support in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is detected
by its underlying <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
Moreover we may restrict to a sufficiently small open subset <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
enabling us to apply Construction <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Construction 2.5 ([, 2.19, 2.20]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">So let <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dots,g_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dots,g_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be functions on <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\cap Y=\bigcap_{i}g_{i}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\cap Y=\bigcap_{i}g_{i}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=Y-g_{i}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=Y-g_{i}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Abusing notation, let <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:Y\cap Z\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:Y\cap Z\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:Y-Z\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:Y-Z\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inclusions.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 2.6 ( [, (4.4.1)]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> there is a distinguished triangle</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{*}i^{!}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y}\to j_{*}j^{*}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to[1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{*}i^{!}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y}\to j_{*}j^{*}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to[1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">inducing a long exact sequence of cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaimx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaimx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Claim</span></span><span id="Thmclaimx2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaimx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaimx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The underlying bifiltered <math id="Thmclaimx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-modules of <math id="Thmclaimx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{Y}(H^{i}j_{*}j^{*}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{Y}(H^{i}j_{*}j^{*}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmclaimx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}|_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}|_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic to each other.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that the Claim has been shown. Then the same argument as in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> in the algebraic case proves the result of the Proposition. Indeed, we have the exact sequence</p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to H^{i}j_{*}j^{*}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to H^{i+1}i_{*}i^{!}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to H^{i}j_{*}j^{*}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})\to H^{i+1}i_{*}i^{!}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i+1}i_{*}i^{!}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i+1}i_{*}i^{!}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> has weights <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq d+n+i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq d+n+i+1</annotation></semantics></math> by Properties <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.F and G. This gives the assertion for <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|Y</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+n</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|Y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}|Y)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the last morphism of the exact sequence is injective so that the assertion follows.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of the Claim.</span>
Let <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}=\pi^{-1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}=\pi^{-1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}_{i}=\pi^{-1}(Y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}_{i}=\pi^{-1}(Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}_{i}=\pi^{*}g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}_{i}=\pi^{*}g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Construction <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Construction 2.5 ([, 2.19, 2.20]). ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>
the associated Čech complex gives a resolution of <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The components of this Čech complex
are direct sums of <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j^{\prime}_{I})_{*}(V^{\rm Hdg}|Y^{\prime}_{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j^{\prime}_{I})_{*}(V^{\rm Hdg}|Y^{\prime}_{I})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}_{I}=\bigcap_{i\in I}Y^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}_{I}=\bigcap_{i\in I}Y^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the inclusion <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}_{I}:Y^{\prime}_{I}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}_{I}:Y^{\prime}_{I}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. By the uniqueness of the open direct image in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sa90</a>, 2.11]</cite> we have moreover</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}(j^{\prime}_{I})_{*}(V^{\rm Hdg}|Y^{\prime}_{I})=(j_{I})_{*}(V^{\rm Hdg}|Y_{I})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}(j^{\prime}_{I})_{*}(V^{\rm Hdg}|Y^{\prime}_{I})=(j_{I})_{*}(V^{\rm Hdg}|Y_{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{I}:Y_{I}:=\bigcap_{i\in I}Y_{i}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{I}:Y_{I}:=\bigcap_{i\in I}Y_{i}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. So we get the desired isomorphism (using the filtration <math id="S5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Dec}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">Dec</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Dec}W</annotation></semantics></math> from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), and Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> follows.∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We return to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> in the analytic case. Applying Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> to <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}=\iota_{X^{\prime}}(\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})=\iota_{X^{\prime}}(V_{X^{\prime}}^{\rm Hdg})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}=\iota_{X^{\prime}}(\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg})=\iota_{X^{\prime}}(V_{X^{\prime}}^{\rm Hdg}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">This implies that we get a morphism <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}:\iota_{X^{\prime}}(V_{X^{\prime}}^{\rm Hdg})\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}:\iota_{X^{\prime}}(V_{X^{\prime}}^{\rm Hdg})\to{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to which we apply <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The decomposition Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 4.6. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> together with the semisimplicity of polarizable Hodge modules imply that <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{X}^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct factor of <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}V_{X^{\prime}}^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}V_{X^{\prime}}^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math>. So we get a morphism</p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})\to{\mathcal{M}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})\to{\mathcal{M}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p">It is not clear whether <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely defined (since the decomposition is not unique). However, its underlying morphism of <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes coincides with the canonically defined adjunction morphism <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\#}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>#</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\#}</annotation></semantics></math> so that it induces the desired morphism of mixed Hodge structures</p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}j^{\#}:IH^{i}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{i}(U,{\mathbb{V}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}j^{\#}:IH^{i}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{i}(U,{\mathbb{V}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a mapping cone of <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})\to{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})\to{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Remember (<a href="#S5.E15" title="In 5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) that <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}_{*}V^{\rm Hdg}</annotation></semantics></math>, a mixed Hodge <em id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">module</em> of weight <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq n+d</annotation></semantics></math> (by Properties <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Properties 2.1. ‣ 2 Mixed Hodge Modules ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. F)) and hence <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;d+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;d+n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by definition of the cone, one has</p>
<table id="S5.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}=0\quad\hbox{for}\,\,\,k&lt;d+n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}=0\quad\hbox{for}\,\,\,k&lt;d+n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Using Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 5.1. ‣ 5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> (e.g. <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}H^{0}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>Hdg</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{X}(V_{X}^{\rm Hdg})=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}H^{0}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>) together with the long exact sequence (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) we get moreover</p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=0\quad\text{for}\,\,\,k\leq i+d+n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=0\quad\text{for}\,\,\,k\leq i+d+n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Since by (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) we have <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}^{i,k}=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i+k}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}^{i,k}=\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i+k}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the weight spectral sequence (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) implies the surjectivity of</p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{matrix}E_{1}^{-d-n-1,d+n+1+j}&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;d_{1}\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;E_{1}^{-d-n,d+n+j+1}\\
\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to12.0pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}&amp;&amp;\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to12.0pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}\\
H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}&amp;\to&amp;H^{j+1}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\end{matrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mtable align="bottom" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo>‖</mo><mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo>‖</mo><mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}E_{1}^{-d-n-1,d+n+1+j}&amp;\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;d_{1}\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}&amp;E_{1}^{-d-n,d+n+j+1}\\
\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to12.0pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}&amp;&amp;\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}\left\|\vbox to12.0pt{}\right.\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}\\
H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}&amp;\to&amp;H^{j+1}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime}\end{matrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.4" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, and this map splits by the semisimplicity of polarizable Hodge modules. So we get the surjectivity of</p>
<table id="S5.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j+1}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\quad\hbox{for any}\,\,i,j." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for any</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j+1}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\quad\hbox{for any}\,\,i,j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaimx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaimx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Claim</span></span><span id="Thmclaimx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaimx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaimx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This implies the surjectivity of</p>
<table id="S5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to H^{i+1}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\quad\hbox{for any}\,\,i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for any</mtext><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to H^{i+1}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\quad\hbox{for any}\,\,i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of the claim.</span> The truncation <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\leq j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\leq j}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> splits by the definition of mixed Hodge complexes so that <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}\simeq\bigoplus_{i}H^{i}(\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime})[-i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≃</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}\simeq\bigoplus_{i}H^{i}(\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime})[-i]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the underlying <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-module of <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the truncation induces a filtration <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the preceding splitting for <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}M^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coming from the truncation induces a splitting for <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}M^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}M^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coming from <math id="S5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its factors are isomorphic to <math id="S5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i-j}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i-j}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n+1}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and this factor maps surjectively to the factor of <math id="S5.p7.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{i+1}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i+1}(a_{X})_{*}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphic to <math id="S5.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i-j}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j+1}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">Gr</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i-j}(a_{X})_{*}H^{j+1}\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{d+n}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Again using <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;d+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;d+n</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S5.E16" title="In 5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) it follows from the weight spectral sequence for <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that</p>
<table id="S5.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=0\quad\text{for}\,\,\,k\leq d+n+i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>Gr</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.510em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Gr}\nolimits^{W}_{k}H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}=0\quad\text{for}\,\,\,k\leq d+n+i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.2" class="ltx_p">The long exact sequence (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) for the direct image of the weakly distinguished triangle of the cone for <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{X}:X\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{X}:X\to</annotation></semantics></math>pt reads</p>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\cdots H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to IH^{i}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;H^{i}(j^{\#})\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}H^{i}(U,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{i+1}(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtable align="bottom" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mi mathsize="0.700em">H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace><mspace style="width:0.28em;" width="0.28em"></mspace></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdots H^{i}(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\to IH^{i}(X,{\mathbb{V}})\mathop{\vbox{\halign{
#\cr${\scriptstyle\hfil\;\;H^{i}(j^{\#})\;\;\hfil}$\crcr\kern 2.0pt\cr\rightarrowfill\crcr}}\;}H^{i}(U,{\mathbb{V}})\to H^{i+1}(a_{X})_{*}{\mathcal{M}}^{\prime\prime}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.3" class="ltx_p">From Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 4.7. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>) this is a sequence of mixed Hodge structures and (<a href="#S5.E17" title="In 5 Proof of Theorem  in the analytic case ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) shows the assertion about the lowest weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">To complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3 Polarizable variations of Hodge structure and the main theorem ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> in the analytic case, we only have to show the independence of the compactification. The map (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) induced by <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from the natural map of <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])\to Rj_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])\to Rj_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math> after applying the global section functor. We may assume that we have a second compactification <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}:U\hookrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}:U\hookrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> related to <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> by a projective morphism <math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The map induced by <math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is similarly obtained from the natural map <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\prime}:Rf_{*}\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])\to Rf_{*}Rj_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]=(Rj^{\prime})_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\prime}:Rf_{*}\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])\to Rf_{*}Rj_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]=(Rj^{\prime})_{*}{\mathbb{V}}[d]</annotation></semantics></math>.
As before, the decomposition Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 4.6. ‣ 4 Mixed Hodge complexes on analytic spaces ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> combined with the fact that polarizable Hodge modules form a semi-simplicial category implies that the intersection complex <math id="S5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{Y}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{Y}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct factor of <math id="S5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{*}\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{*}\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{X}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math> (but the latter might contain other direct factors). The restriction of <math id="S5.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{Y}({\mathbb{V}}[d])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{{\mathcal{I}}{\mathcal{C}}}\nolimits_{Y}({\mathbb{V}}[d])</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly equal to <math id="S5.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> while the other direct factors are in the kernel of <math id="S5.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ι</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> since these must be supported on <math id="S5.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y-U</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that the image of <math id="S5.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on the compactification and hence neither does the image of (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Remark 1.2. ‣ 1 Perverse sheaves ‣ Lowest Weights in Cohomology of Variations of Hodge Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
